==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰeydʰ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|abyden}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ābīdan||to abide, wait, remain, delay, remain behind; survive; wait for, await; expect}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*uzbīdaną||to expect, tolerate}}, equivalent to {{prefix|en|a|bide}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|abide||to abide, remain}}, {{cog|gmh|erbīten||to await, expect}}, {{cog|got|𐌿𐍃𐌱𐌴𐌹𐌳𐌰𐌽||to expect, await, have patience}}. The sense of ''pay for'' is due to influence from {{m|en|aby}}.<ref>{{R:MW3 1976|page=3}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈbaɪd/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈbaɪd/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-abide.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-ca-abide.ogg|Audio (CA)}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪd|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|abides|abiding|abode|past2=abided|past3=abid|past_ptc2=abided|past_ptc3=abidden|past_ptc3_qual=rare}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To endure without yielding; to [[withstand]]. {{defdate|from mid-12th c.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{syn|en|hold on|resist|persevere|Thesaurus:persevere}}
#: {{ux|en|The old oak tree '''abides''' the wind endlessly.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bear [[patiently]]. {{defdate|from late 15th c.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{syn|en|brook|put up with|tolerate|Thesaurus:tolerate}}
#: {{uxi|en|"I never could '''abide''' shoemakers," said an old servant,—and it ended in her marrying one.|ref=<ref name="Holland"/>}}
#* {{RQ:Jack Straw|III|59|VVe will be Kings and Lords within our ſelues,<br>And not '''abide''' the pride of tyrranie.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2|act=III|scene=ii|page=87|column=2|passage=Neuer neuer: ſhe would alwayes ſay ſhee could not '''abide''' M[aster] ''Shallow''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=02 Dec 78|volume=6|number=19|journal=Gay Community News|page=14|title=!!HELP!! (personal advertisement)|text=We are vegetarian leaning, dislike smoking and alcohol, cannot '''abide''' drugs.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|title=The Big Lebowski|year=1998|medium=motion picture|writers=Joel Coen; Ethan Coen|role=The Big Lebowski|actor=David Huddleston |passage=By God sir. I will not '''abide''' another toe.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To pay for; to stand the consequences of. {{defdate|from late 16th c.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{syn|en|answer for|suffer|atone}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer|act=III|scene=ii|page=154|column=2|passage=Diſparage not the faith thou doſt not know, / Leſt to thy perill thou '''abide''' it deare.|brackets=on}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|III|ii|122|1|If it be found ſo, ſome will deere '''abide''' it.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|IV|Ay me, they little know / How dearly I '''abide''' that boaſt ſo vaine, / Under what torments inwardly I groane{{...}}}}
# {{used in phrasal verbs|lang=en|abide by|t=to accept and act in accordance with}}
#: {{ux|en|The new teacher was strict and the students did not want to '''abide''' by his rules.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[wait]] in expectation. {{defdate|mid-12th|mid-17th c.}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=4}}</ref>
#: {{syn|en|hold on|stay|Thesaurus:wait}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Genesis|22|5||And Abraham ſaid vnto his yong men, '''Abide''' you here with the aſſe, and I and the lad will goe yonder and worſhip, and come againe to you.|url=https://archive.org/stream/Bible1611/Binder1#page/n98}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[pause]]; to [[delay]]. {{defdate|from ca. 1150—1350 to mid-17th c.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# {{lb|en|intransitive|archaic|Scotland}} To [[stay]]; to [[continue]] in a place; to remain [[stable]] or [[fixed]] in some state or condition; to be left. {{defdate|from ca. 1150—1350}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{RQ:KJV|Ecclesiastes|1|4|passage=One generation passeth away, and another generation commeth: but the earth '''abideth''' for euer.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|1 Corinthians|7|20||Let euery man '''abide''' in the ſame calling wherein he was called.|url=https://archive.org/stream/Bible1611/Binder1#page/n1405}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Luke|24|29|passage=But they constrained him, saying, '''Abide''' with vs, for it is towards euening, and the day is farre spent: And he went in, to tarrie with them.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|archaic}} To have one's [[abode]]. {{defdate|from ca. 1350—1470}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{syn|en|dwell|live|reside|Thesaurus:reside}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Genesis|24|55||And her brother and her mother ſaid, Let the damſell '''abide''' with vs a few dayes, at the leaſt ten ; after that, ſhe ſhall goe.|url=https://archive.org/stream/Bible1611/Binder1#page/n102}}
#* {{RQ:Beckett Watt|text=In empty hush, in airless gloom, Mr. Knott '''abode''', in the large room set aside for his exclusive enjoyment, and that of his attendant.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|archaic}} To endure; to [[remain]]; to last. {{defdate|from ca. 1350—1470}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{quote-av|en|year=1998|role=Narrator|medium=motion picture|writers=Joel and Ethan Coen|actor=w:Sam Elliot|title=The Big Lebowski|passage=The Dude '''abides'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To stand ready for; to await for someone; watch for. {{defdate|from early 12th c.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{syn|en|await|wait for|Thesaurus:wait for}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Acts|20|23||Saue that the holy Ghoſt witneſſeth in euery city, ſaying that bonds and afflictions '''abide''' me.|url=https://archive.org/stream/Bible1611/Binder1#page/n1374}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Idylls|page=52|passage=I will '''abide''' the coming of my lord.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[endure]] or [[undergo]] a hard trial or a task; to stand up under. {{defdate|from ca. 1150—1350 to early 18th c.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Idylls|page=31|passage={{...}}And shalt '''abide''' her judgment on it.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To await [[submissive|submissively]]; accept without question; submit to. {{defdate|from ca. 1350—1470}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|act=V|scene=vi|page=45|passage=The grand Conſpirator, Abbot of Weſtminſter, / With clog of Conſcience, and ſowre Melancholly, / Hath yeelded vp his body to the graue : / But here is ''Carlile,'' liuing to '''abide''' / Thy Kingly doome, and ſentence of his pride.}}

====Usage notes====
* {{sense|bear patiently}} The negative form '''can't abide''' is used to indicate strong dislike.

